{"date": "2026-11-04", "post_id": 1507408, "title": "The Not-So-Hidden Galaxy, IC 342", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-4-the-not-so-hidden-galaxy-ic-342/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> \u201cI see\u201d IC 342, the Hidden Galaxy! <a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/u/ArtemM?i=imsupt\">Today\u2019s image</a> peers through the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2019-september-27-the-annotated-galactic-center/\">plane of the Milky Way</a>, through all its gas, dust and stars, to unveil this <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/galaxies/types/\">spiral galaxy</a>. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-january-8-ic-342-hidden-galaxy-in-camelopardalis/\">IC 342</a> (or <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/explore-the-night-sky/hubble-caldwell-catalog/caldwell-5/\">Caldwell 5</a>) is one of the brightest members of the IC 342/<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2010-march-9-galaxies-beyond-the-heart-maffei-1-and-2/\">Maffei</a> group of galaxies. Located about 10 million <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/what-is-a-light-year/\">light years</a> away, this group is quite close to the <a href=\"https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/features/cosmic/local_group_info.html\">Local Group</a> that includes our Milky Way. Inaccurate accounting of the crowded and dusty Milky Way foreground has led to misunderstandings in IC 342\u2019s history, including the theory that the galaxy was <a href=\"https://articles.adsabs.harvard.edu/pdf/1993AJ....105..886V\">ejected from the Local Group</a>. IC 342 has its own dust intermittently blocking the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-january-30-hydrogen-clouds-of-m33/\">glow of hydrogen gas</a>, indicating the presence of bright, young stars. <a href=\"https://www.mpg.de/9283089/magnetic-field-galaxy-IC342\">Magnetic field maps</a> of IC 342 reveal that magnetic fields may contribute substantially to one of the biggest galactic puzzles: <a href=\"https://www.universetoday.com/articles/astronomers-are-still-trying-to-understand-how-a-galaxys-spiral-arms-form\">how do spiral arms form</a>?<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> open space", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/artofomin/\">Artem Mironov</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/artofomin/\">Artem Mironov</a>", "alt": "The face of a spiral galaxy fills the image. The galaxy has a bright yellow-ish center. A handful of large arms spiral outward from the center. Bubbles of red glowing gas and tendrils of dark dust trace the spiral arms. Foreground stars and background galaxies are sprinkled across the image.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-4-the-not-so-hidden-galaxy-ic-342/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/ic342.png?w=4144&h=2822&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 4 - The Not-So-Hidden Galaxy, IC 342</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-4-the-not-so-hidden-galaxy-ic-342/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: \u201cI see\u201d IC 342, the Hidden Galaxy! Today\u2019s image peers through the plane of the Milky Way, through all its gas, dust and stars, to unveil this spiral galaxy. IC 342 (or Caldwell 5) is one of the brightest members of the IC 342/Maffei group of galaxies. Located about 10 million light years away, this group is quite close to the Local Group that includes our Milky Way. Inaccurate accounting of the crowded and dusty Milky Way foreground has led to misunderstandings in IC 342\u2019s history, including the theory that the galaxy was ejected from the Local Group. IC 342 has its own dust intermittently blocking the glow of hydrogen gas, indicating the presence of bright, young stars. Magnetic field maps of IC 342 reveal that magnetic fields may contribute substantially to one of the biggest galactic puzzles: how do spiral arms form?Tomorrow&#039;s picture: open space\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"the not-so-hidden galaxy, ic 342\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"The Not-So-Hidden Galaxy, IC 342\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-4-the-not-so-hidden-galaxy-ic-342/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/ic342.png?w=4144&#038;h=2822&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/ic342.png?w=4144&#038;h=2822&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"The face of a spiral galaxy fills the image. The galaxy has a bright yellow-ish center. A handful of large arms spiral outward from the center. Bubbles of red glowing gas and tendrils of dark dust trace the spiral arms. Foreground stars and background galaxies are sprinkled across the image.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-04T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-03T11:06:01-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"The Not-So-Hidden Galaxy, IC 342\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/ic342.png?w=4144&#038;h=2822&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 4<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/ic342.png\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/ic342.png\" alt=\"The face of a spiral galaxy fills the image. The galaxy has a bright yellow-ish center. A handful of large arms spiral outward from the center. Bubbles of red glowing gas and tendrils of dark dust trace the spiral arms. Foreground stars and background galaxies are sprinkled across the image.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> The Not-So-Hidden Galaxy, IC 342 </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/artofomin/\">Artem Mironov</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> \u201cI see\u201d IC 342, the Hidden Galaxy! <a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/u/ArtemM?i=imsupt\">Today\u2019s image</a> peers through the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2019-september-27-the-annotated-galactic-center/\">plane of the Milky Way</a>, through all its gas, dust and stars, to unveil this <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/galaxies/types/\">spiral galaxy</a>. <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-january-8-ic-342-hidden-galaxy-in-camelopardalis/\">IC 342</a> (or <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/hubble/science/explore-the-night-sky/hubble-caldwell-catalog/caldwell-5/\">Caldwell 5</a>) is one of the brightest members of the IC 342/<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2010-march-9-galaxies-beyond-the-heart-maffei-1-and-2/\">Maffei</a> group of galaxies. Located about 10 million <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/what-is-a-light-year/\">light years</a> away, this group is quite close to the <a href=\"https://imagine.gsfc.nasa.gov/features/cosmic/local_group_info.html\">Local Group</a> that includes our Milky Way. Inaccurate accounting of the crowded and dusty Milky Way foreground has led to misunderstandings in IC 342\u2019s history, including the theory that the galaxy was <a href=\"https://articles.adsabs.harvard.edu/pdf/1993AJ....105..886V\">ejected from the Local Group</a>. IC 342 has its own dust intermittently blocking the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-january-30-hydrogen-clouds-of-m33/\">glow of hydrogen gas</a>, indicating the presence of bright, young stars. <a href=\"https://www.mpg.de/9283089/magnetic-field-galaxy-IC342\">Magnetic field maps</a> of IC 342 reveal that magnetic fields may contribute substantially to one of the biggest galactic puzzles: <a href=\"https://www.universetoday.com/articles/astronomers-are-still-trying-to-understand-how-a-galaxys-spiral-arms-form\">how do spiral arms form</a>?<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> open space\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-3-comet-panstarrs-and-rainbow-meteor/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nKeighley Rockcliffe, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261104/html"}